{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"With a key-set prev, keys present in both prev and curr route to `_compute_mutations_emit_both`\nwith an empty `local_fields`, producing ~516 mutations per pass that are provably inert but not free.\nSkip them. session_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4** (measured facts; do not re-derive).\n\n## Why\nEach inert mutation still drives one per-mutation loop iteration in the batch apply path\n(`applier.py:639-640`), which calls `_recheck_drift` ->\n`concurrency.snapshot_head(repo_root)` — **a `git rev-parse` subprocess** — before dispatching via `_apply_one`. So ~516 extra\ngit invocations per pass, ~516 `RECON: batch_outcome` stderr lines, ~516 outcome records in the\nmanifest, and an inflated `mutation_count` so a converged pass reports 516 instead of 0. It also\nenlarges the HEAD-drift window. Under `BOOTSTRAP_STRICT` (cap 10) or `BOOTSTRAP_THROTTLE` (cap 100)\nthese compete for cap slots with genuine work (`apply_planning.py:90-101`) and can starve it —\nproduction defaults to `LIVE` (cap `None`), so that risk is latent, not live.\n\n## Approach\nTreat the empty dict written by the key-set prev snapshot as a snapshot-route marker, not as a\ncanonical local ticket with every Jira field cleared. Add an explicit opt-in flag to\n`compute_mutations` for the snapshot-key-set caller in `run_differs.py`; when that flag is set,\n`_compute_mutations_emit_both` skips branch-3 update emission for keys whose local-side value is\nempty. The default `compute_mutations(local_state, jira_state)` behavior remains unchanged for\ncanonical local-vs-Jira callers, including genuine empty-local clear/update semantics.\n\nConcrete examples: with the opt-in set, key-set prev `{\"PROJ-1\": {}}` plus current Jira fields\nemits no branch-3 update, so no inert mutation enters the pass tally/apply path. Without the opt-in,\nthe same pair still emits the existing canonical clear/update. Parent-only and reporter-only changes\nalso remain emitted in the default canonical path.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] With a key-set prev over a real corpus, `(outbound, update)` mutations with an empty effective\n      payload are **0** (down from ~516). Tier: **unit** on `compute_mutations` + the corpus script.\n- [ ] Mutations with a genuine non-empty effective payload are still emitted unchanged, including\n      parent-only and reporter-only branch-3 changes that route through dedicated outbound sub-calls\n      before scalar filtering — the negative controls. Tier: **unit**.\n- [ ] `mutation_count` on a converged pass is 0. Tier: **integration** asserting the pass record.\n- [ ] `git rev-parse` invocation count per pass equals the number of actually emitted mutations (0 on\n      the converged-pass case in AC3). Tier: **integration** with a counting shim.\n\n## Files\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/differ.py`, `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/_field_contract.py`, `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_apply_phases.py`, `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/run_differs.py`, tests\n\nScope: S. Depends on A4-2."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788882692670300001,"uuid":"9412e874-29d5-42d6-9c00-9b5ccf8ece8b"}